From 3684522a1188718c72a7b18ef3ee708006607665 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dominggoes Isakh <drjisakh@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 09:59:44 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 10/14] BpfNetMaps: guard uid permission chunk map access
 against missing BPF

Bootanimation now gets further than ever (past the earlier power HAL
and AdvancedProtectionService fixes), but system_server is still
crash-looping - this time on ConnectivityServiceInitializer, 3 times
in the captured logcat with the identical trace:

  RuntimeException: Failed to create service ConnectivityServiceInitializer
  Caused by: NullPointerException: Attempt to invoke interface method
    '...IBpfMap.getValue(...)' on a null object reference
    at BpfNetMaps.setChunkPermListForUids(BpfNetMaps.java:1606)
    at PermissionMonitor.lambda$new$0(PermissionMonitor.java:503)
    ... at ConnectivityService.<init>(ConnectivityService.java:2634)

Root cause: this device's 3.10 kernel predates eBPF entirely, so this
tree already carries a local "private static final boolean hasBpf =
false;" (BpfNetMaps.java:177) to make BPF-dependent code degrade
gracefully. initBpfMaps() respects that: getConfigurationMap() returns
null when hasBpf is false, and the very first line of initBpfMaps()
is "if (sConfigurationMap == null) return;" - so the whole method
bails before ever reaching getUidPermissionChunkMap(), leaving
sUidPermissionChunkMap permanently null. That's consistent with the
rest of the class's own convention: removeRule() already does
"if (sUidOwnerMap == null) return;" for exactly this reason.

The problem is that sPermissionMapUidMigrationEnabled (which gates
throwIfUidMigrationIsDisabled()) is set independently of hasBpf, so
"migration enabled" and "chunk map actually initialized" can disagree
on a no-BPF device: migration reads as enabled, the early-exit guard
that every other map getter in this file already has was simply never
added to the three call sites that touch sUidPermissionChunkMap
directly (setChunkPermListForUids, forEachUidPermission,
getChunkPermForUid - the latter two reachable from
removePermissionsForUserId/dumpUidPermissionChunkMap too). Added the
same null-check-and-no-op pattern to all three, matching removeRule()'s
existing convention instead of introducing a new one.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Change-Id: I309b036fc196e61dc0b02f95a5a60175ac9e98ef
---
 service/src/com/android/server/BpfNetMaps.java | 9 +++++++++
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+)

diff --git a/service/src/com/android/server/BpfNetMaps.java b/service/src/com/android/server/BpfNetMaps.java
index 11bc3851af..1bc50c13a6 100644
--- a/service/src/com/android/server/BpfNetMaps.java
+++ b/service/src/com/android/server/BpfNetMaps.java
@@ -1589,6 +1589,13 @@ public class BpfNetMaps {
             "setChunkPermListForUids is not available when flag permission_map_uid_migration" +
             " is disabled");
 
+        // On devices without BPF support, initBpfMaps() returns early (see the
+        // sConfigurationMap == null check) before ever reaching
+        // getUidPermissionChunkMap(), so sUidPermissionChunkMap stays null even though
+        // migration itself is considered "enabled". Same no-op-on-missing-map convention
+        // already used by removeRule()'s sUidOwnerMap check above.
+        if (sUidPermissionChunkMap == null) return;
+
         // group UIDs by chunkId
         final ArrayMap<Integer, ArraySet<Integer>> uidsByChunkId = groupPermissionsIdsBy(
             permissionsUids,
@@ -1771,6 +1778,7 @@ public class BpfNetMaps {
     private void forEachUidPermission(
         ThrowingBiConsumer<Integer, Integer> action
     ) throws ErrnoException {
+        if (sUidPermissionChunkMap == null) return;
         sUidPermissionChunkMap.forEach((chunkId, chunk) -> {
             for(int index = 0; index < chunk.val.length; index++) {
                 int shift = 0;
@@ -2110,6 +2118,7 @@ public class BpfNetMaps {
         throwIfUidMigrationIsDisabled(
             "getChunkPermForUid is not available when flag" +
             " permission_map_uid_migration is disabled");
+        if (sUidPermissionChunkMap == null) return PERMISSION_BIT_NONE;
         try {
             UidPermissionChunk uidPermissionChunk = sUidPermissionChunkMap.getValue(
                 new S32(getChunkId(uid)));
